labels.description:
Most of what Dayline does is not configured here. It is decided by two labels you apply in [Settings → Areas & labels](/config/labels) and by `#tags` you type into your own calendar event titles — so it lives where the thing itself lives, and changes without a restart.

**Want a calendar on the card?** Give it the **{include}** label.
Right now the spine comes from {how}:

{calendars}

**Want an event to *do* something?** Put a `#tag` in its title — `Dinner out #Away` — and give that calendar the **{control}** label as well. Without the second label the chip still shows and nothing fires; that is deliberate, so a `#vacation` in a school calendar you subscribe to cannot touch your house.

Allowed to act:

{controls}

Then bind the tag to whatever you like with the **Dayline tag** blueprint (Settings → Automations → Blueprints → Import, `https://github.com/bitmux/Dayline/blob/main/blueprints/automation/dayline/dayline_tag.yaml`). The binding is an ordinary automation you can open and extend, so “unless someone is still home” is yours to write. Dayline fires the `dayline_tag` event and stops there.

Tags seen on today's events: {tags}

**Want to be told when something changes on its own?** Give that entity the **{include}** label too — the same label, because on anything that is not a calendar it means the other thing. It gets a plain sentence from its own name; write a better one under “What just happened”.

Explained when they change:

{watched}

A tag fires once, at its event's start — never at the end. Calendars are read every {scan} minutes, so a tag added to an event starting in the next minute or two may not fire.
